[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-77515"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072133/assets/c800644be3cc0ad0688d52948a4a2cb813cbe14b1fe899abe5a1d92613a687bf.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072133/assets/90272fad8af526de6e169faba8daae29210c1f502c4959d0e1829e5ad65476f0.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=2|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=2p.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=4|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ and $q$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=4q.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =2p+4q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=2p+4q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =4$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-120277/selection/85ef7f50523b0629b440aabdf7aa4e5d495348cb257429b219acd95af404db7f" :answer/id #uuid "8706a670-9736-5b74-b628-6b1c4e188e98" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =4$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120277/selection/68dafec87ec1088dd94dca3f37df0f36d5c6c11e4dc5c7bb0a6944484883428f" :answer/id #uuid "5281798b-85c1-5eaa-9e3d-f3f3b8be0df4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-4$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120277/selection/8f5980eefd2afc60c5673e776d4290f0c9e52a2520a811e95c725e4abda4ee34" :answer/id #uuid "824d7e30-9199-503e-94b1-de0ae40b73b2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\lambda =4$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-120277/selection/e6734f46800a8b3f7eee7a03c9ae55defec1d5